---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import {i18nSidebar} from '../../../data/i18n-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
import {dep} from '../../../data/site';
---

<DocsLayout title="Getting Started" sidebar={i18nSidebar}
            description="Add the dependency, write a catalog, resolve a message">

    <h1>Getting Started</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        A catalog, an <code>I18n</code> instance, and a resolved string.
    </p>

    <h2>Dependency</h2>

    <Code code={`dependencies {
    implementation("${dep.i18n}")
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>The runtime is multiplatform and has no dependencies of its own.</p>

    <h2>The runtime, without codegen</h2>

    <p>You can use the resolver directly. This is what the generated accessors call underneath, and it is
        the quickest way to see the moving parts.</p>

    <Code code={`import io.peekandpoke.ultra.i18n.I18n
import io.peekandpoke.ultra.i18n.Locale
import io.peekandpoke.ultra.i18n.MapI18nCatalog

val catalog = MapI18nCatalog(
    "en" to mapOf(
        "checkout.greeting" to "Hello {{name}}!",
        "checkout.items_one" to "{{count}} item",
        "checkout.items_other" to "{{count}} items",
    ),
    "de" to mapOf(
        "checkout.greeting" to "Hallo {{name}}!",
    ),
)

val i18n = I18n(locale = Locale("de"), fallback = Locale("en")) {
    install(catalog)
}

i18n.resolve("checkout.greeting", mapOf("name" to "Alice"))
// -> "Hallo Alice!"

i18n.resolvePlural("checkout.items", count = 3)
// -> "3 items"   (no German plural in the catalog, so the chain falls through to en)`}
          lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Two things to note</h2>

    <p><strong>The fallback is required, and it is not derived from the locale.</strong> It has to be a
        concrete target language you know is complete. Deriving it from <code>locale.base</code> would leave
        <code>de-CH</code> falling back to <code>de</code> and then to nothing.</p>

    <p><strong>A missing key returns the key itself</strong> rather than throwing or rendering empty. A
        visible <code>checkout.greeting</code> in the UI is a bug report; a blank space is not.</p>

    <h2>Placeholders</h2>

    <p>Placeholders are <code>{'{{name}}'}</code>. Substitution is <strong>single-pass</strong>: a value is
        never re-scanned, so a value that itself contains <code>{'{{...}}'}</code> can never be
        re-interpreted as another placeholder.</p>

    <Code code={`i18n.resolve("greeting", mapOf("name" to "{{secret}}", "secret" to "hunter2"))
// -> "Hello {{secret}}!"   — NOT "Hello hunter2!"`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>That matters when any part of a message comes from user input. Unknown placeholders are left in place
        for the same reason as missing keys &mdash; visibly wrong beats silently blank.</p>

    <h2>Plurals</h2>

    <p>Plural forms are separate keys with a <code>_one</code> / <code>_other</code> suffix.
        <code>resolvePlural</code> picks the form and supplies <code>{'{{count}}'}</code> for you.</p>

    <Code code={`i18n.resolvePlural("checkout.items", count = 1)   // "1 item"
i18n.resolvePlural("checkout.items", count = 7)   // "7 items"`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>If the <code>_one</code> form is missing the <code>_other</code> form is used, and if both are
        missing the base key is returned. Selection currently uses the simple <code>count == 1</code> rule
        rather than CLDR plural categories.</p>

    <h2>Next</h2>

    <p>Writing catalogs by hand gets old quickly, and it puts the key strings back in your code. The Gradle
        plugin generates typed accessors from YAML and checks your languages against each other.</p>

    <div class="not-prose mt-8">
        <a href="/ultra/i18n/catalogs"
           class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 bg-brand-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-brand-500 transition-colors">
            Catalogs &amp; codegen &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>

</DocsLayout>
